1. **A microphone assembly (1)** attachable to a user’s clothing, comprising:  

   - a **clip (2)** configured to releasably attach the microphone assembly (1) to the clothing of the user;  
   - at least two **microphones (3a, 3b)** arranged to receive acoustic signals and to generate corresponding microphone signals;  
   - an **acceleration sensor (4)** configured to generate acceleration data indicative of movement and/or orientation of the microphone assembly (1);  
   - a **processing unit (5)** operatively connected to the microphones (3a, 3b) and to the acceleration sensor (4);  
   - a **beamformer (6)** implemented by or connected to the processing unit (5), the beamformer (6) being configured to process the microphone signals to provide a directional audio signal;  
   - a **direction-of-arrival unit (7)** configured to estimate a direction of arrival of sound based on the microphone signals; and  
   - a **control unit (8)** configured to control at least one operating parameter of the beamformer (6) in dependence on the acceleration data and/or the estimated direction of arrival.  

2. **The microphone assembly (1) according to claim 1**, wherein the control unit (8) is configured to adapt a steering direction, beam width, gain, noise suppression parameter, or microphone weighting of the beamformer (6).  

3. **The microphone assembly (1) according to claim 1 or 2**, wherein the acceleration sensor (4) is configured to detect a change in wearing position of the microphone assembly (1), and wherein the control unit (8) is configured to compensate for the detected change by modifying the directional processing of the beamformer (6).  

4. **The microphone assembly (1) according to any preceding claim**, wherein the direction-of-arrival unit (7) is configured to distinguish between speech arriving from a target direction and noise arriving from a non-target direction.  

5. **The microphone assembly (1) according to any preceding claim**, wherein the processing unit (5) is configured to output the directional audio signal to a hearing device, communication device, recording device, or wireless transmitter.

A wearable microphone apparatus, comprising:

a housing configured to be removably attached to an article of clothing of a user;

a plurality of microphones supported by the housing, the plurality of microphones comprising at least three microphones configured to generate respective microphone signals;

an orientation sensor supported by the housing, the orientation sensor comprising an acceleration sensor configured to sense acceleration due to gravity along at least two axes and to generate orientation information indicative of an orientation of the housing relative to gravity;

an audio processing circuit configured to receive the microphone signals and to perform at least one of beamforming or direction-of-arrival detection based on the microphone signals; and

a controller operatively coupled to the orientation sensor and the audio processing circuit, the controller being configured to determine, based on the orientation information, an orientation state of the housing and to restrict a beam steering angle used by the audio processing circuit to a permitted angular range corresponding to the determined orientation state.

In some embodiments, the plurality of microphones are arranged at different positions on the housing to define a microphone array. The audio processing circuit may be configured to form a directional pickup beam using signals from the at least three microphones and to steer the directional pickup beam toward a detected sound source direction. The controller may limit the steering of the directional pickup beam such that, when the housing is worn in an expected orientation on the clothing of the user, the directional pickup beam is prevented from being steered outside a predetermined forward-facing angular region.

In some embodiments, the acceleration sensor is a two-axis or three-axis accelerometer. The controller may determine a tilt angle, roll angle, pitch angle, or other orientation parameter of the housing based on the acceleration due to gravity sensed along the at least two axes. The permitted angular range may be selected as a function of the determined tilt or orientation of the housing.

In some embodiments, the audio processing circuit performs direction-of-arrival detection to estimate an arrival direction of speech or other target audio relative to the microphone array. The controller may compare the estimated arrival direction with the permitted angular range and may cause the beamforming to steer toward the estimated arrival direction only when the estimated arrival direction lies within the permitted angular range. When the estimated arrival direction lies outside the permitted angular range, the controller may inhibit steering toward the estimated arrival direction, limit the steering angle to a boundary of the permitted angular range, or select a default steering direction.

In some embodiments, the housing includes a clip, clasp, magnet, pin, or other attachment structure for attaching the microphone apparatus to clothing. The controller may compensate for variations in the manner in which the housing is attached to the clothing by adjusting the permitted angular range according to the orientation information generated by the acceleration sensor.

A method of operating a wearable microphone apparatus, comprising:

receiving, from at least three microphones supported by a housing configured to be clipped to clothing of a user, respective microphone signals;

sensing, with an acceleration sensor supported by the housing, acceleration due to gravity along at least two axes;

determining, based on the sensed acceleration due to gravity, an orientation of the housing relative to gravity;

performing direction-of-arrival detection based on the microphone signals to estimate an arrival direction of sound;

selecting, based on the determined orientation of the housing, a permitted beam steering angular range; and

performing beamforming on the microphone signals while restricting a beam steering angle to the permitted beam steering angular range.

In some embodiments, restricting the beam steering angle comprises preventing steering of a directional pickup beam toward a rearward or downward direction relative to the user when the orientation of the housing indicates that the housing is attached to the clothing in a normal wearing position. In other embodiments, restricting the beam steering angle comprises mapping an estimated direction-of-arrival into a coordinate system referenced to gravity and constraining the mapped direction-of-arrival according to a predetermined user-facing angular region.

A non-transitory computer-readable medium may store instructions which, when executed by one or more processors of the wearable microphone apparatus, cause the one or more processors to receive microphone signals from at least three microphones, receive acceleration information from an acceleration sensor sensing gravity in at least two axes, determine an orientation of a clothing-mounted housing, perform direction-of-arrival detection and beamforming, and limit a beam steering angle based on the determined orientation.
